      "math_academy_id": "e-5452",
      "problem": "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14242551/assets/05afabfd34db9dbf5bc21b39fd9c7c0b2980e049c65d2f92d42b422799325a69.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14242551/assets/8ffbdf92b1bfb205b70506f0cc394442e000f01787a52861628225fb7a75f1ad.svg) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14242551/assets/30f597293e89dd4f259aad6482f55ce6d68c40575952e139edf8958174455370.svg)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14242551/assets/bae978dbe38dff6676abb57d2e827454cfdae3a0be8d588565bc50f89a5074d2.svg)",
